  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1017 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nTuesday through Saturday:\n\nSynopsis: Cool advection will begin in earnest in the low levels on \nTuesday as Monday evening's cold front continues into the Ohio River \nValley. Cold advection will be further augmented by a secondary \nnortherly wind shift/cold frontal feature during the afternoon \nhours, which will act to draw an air mass from the Hudson Bay \nvicinity into southern Wisconsin for the middle part of the week. \nThe passing secondary surface boundary could trigger a few pop-up \nthunderstorms during the peak heating of the day on Tuesday, with \neast-central and southeast Wisconsin being most favored for activity \nat this time. High pressure will build from the northern Great \nPlains into the western Great Lakes Wednesday through Thursday, \nmaintaing a cooler and drier weather pattern. The high will shift \neast Friday into Saturday, allowing for southerly winds and steadily \nclimbing surface temperatures during the conclusion of the period. \nIncreasing humidity will eventually accompany the rising \ntemperatures, though its arrival will lag behind that of the warmer \nair temperatures. Widespread muggy conditions will thus hold off \nuntil Saturday night or the daytime hours Sunday. \n\nTuesday afternoon: Will be monitoring for a few pop-up thunderstorms \nas a secondary wind shift/cold front pulls south through the area. \nDevelopment of activity will be contingent upon sufficient low level \nmoisture behind Monday's initial cold front, which as of this \nforecast appears likely to be in place. East-central and southeast \nWisconsin are currently most favored for activity, given maximized \nlow level lift from the passing surface boundary and any afternoon \nlake breeze. Severe weather potential is low in any thunderstorms, \nthough an isolated storm with stronger wind gusts can't be entirely \nruled out. Will continue to refine favored precip corridors and \nhazards potential over coming forecasts.\n\nQuigley",
